id: design.idempotency
kind: design
title: Idempotency
aliases: idempotent, idempotency key, safe to retry
tags: architecture, reliability, api
questions:
  - how do you make an operation safe to retry?
  - how do you avoid double-processing a payment?
invariant: An idempotent operation has the same effect whether it runs once or many times, which is what makes retries, redelivery and reconciliation safe.
answer_30s: Make the operation addressable by a caller-supplied idempotency key, store the result of the first execution, and return that stored result for every repeat instead of acting again.
answer_2m: |
  Idempotency is the property that lets everything else work: retries after a
  timeout (the write may have succeeded), at-least-once queues (redelivery is
  normal), reconciliation loops (re-running is expected), and crash recovery
  (resume from stored state). Three implementation shapes, in increasing order
  of generality: naturally idempotent operations (set-to-value, PUT, upsert),
  keyed deduplication (unique constraint or key table on the request id, storing
  the response), and effectidempotent design (each step records that it
  happened, so replay skips completed steps). The interview trap is claiming
  "we retry, so it is safe" -- the retry is only safe because of one of the three
  above, and the key table needs its own retention policy.
when_to_use:
  - any write reachable by retry, queue redelivery, controller reconciliation or user double-click
minimal_example: |
  POST /payments   Idempotency-Key: 8f3c...

  begin
      insert into idempotency(key, request_hash)      -- fails if key exists
      perform the effect
      update idempotency set response = ..., status = 'done'
  commit
  -- a replay with the same key returns the stored response, never a second effect
failure_modes:
  - a retry after a timeout that duplicates the write (no key, no unique constraint)
  - a deduplication key without retention, so the table grows without bound
  - deduplication keyed on the request body instead of a caller-generated id, so a legitimate repeat is treated as a duplicate
follow_ups:
  - what if the first attempt failed halfway?
related:
  - design.retry-backoff
  - design.exactly-once
  - design.state-machine
  - k8s.reconciliation
  - k8s.job
  - recipe.async-job-processing
sources:
  - https://stripe.com/docs/api/idempotent_requests
  - https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/making-retries-safe-with-idempotent-APIs/
